7. One test read closely

  test_store_byte_unchanged_on_payload_tamper (0d398f6, test_shuttle.py, lines
  ~120-140).
  Left: dir_snapshot(self.proposals_dir) taken before the import attempt, a dict
  of relative path -> raw bytes for every regular file under the store.
  Right: dir_snapshot(self.proposals_dir) after the attempt raised, compared with
  assertEqual(before, after).
  Where each side comes from: both read the store directory on disk, before and
  after the call. Neither side is computed by shuttle.py, so this is a
  before/after comparison of the world the code acts on, not code compared with
  itself. It is the work order's property expressed as dict equality; my oracle
  expresses the same property as one hashlib digest plus a file count, as the
  work order asked.
  The test also asserts "sha256" is in the error text, which pins the reason for
  the rejection, not only that something was rejected. Under P3 (hash comparison
  removed) it is this assertion that goes red first, through the missing raise.

